\alias{TwapExecution}
\title{An execution that sends equal slices at even intervals}
\description{
The \code{twap} execution of a plan: equal slices sent on a clock, a time-weighted average price order.

The quantity is cut into \code{slices}, from 2 to 60, and one is sent every \code{over_minutes} times 60 divided by \code{slices} seconds, the first at once. Units that do not divide evenly go to the earliest slices, each slice is worked out from the order's total when it falls due, so a total a join changes is spread over the slices still to come, and a slice that has not filled is left resting when the next goes. Slices are whole lots, and a slice that comes to nothing is skipped rather than stalling the order. The order starts working as soon as its trigger holds.

A TWAP can be the outer execution of a nested pair, releasing slices that an inner \code{IcebergExecution}, \code{VwapExecution}, \code{FrontLoadedExecution} or another TWAP works, or the inner one, working each slice of an outer execution. It cannot carry a resting stop, because a stop protects the whole position at once.
}
